\\})=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "17a473c6-6c8d-5968-aa6a-8059d32a39f1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "816648e9-966e-5cf2-839c-30024fe1baea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449844} {:answer/id #uuid "5555ba44-b062-5e8b-b183-16129f0fc2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449845}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449845} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a0fd59-a9ee-584e-8be4-712573413f2c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a963f06c-c986-5d91-9ccc-f663b195f77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449846} {:answer/id #uuid "f9054fa5-b0f9-5ece-93e7-1ee2ccb1796b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449846} :answer-field/id #uuid "c53ef9e0-ce91-5531-b56d-d801d314355e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0872989-936e-53ad-80f1-db5035c605ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449848} {:answer/id #uuid "355ac780-1b2e-598e-a421-8ddc9710eaf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449848} :answer-field/id #uuid "adc6cc33-91be-5597-916f-94e4653b4dba" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ffee0ea-a323-5b51-850e-98d4c4b547d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159784" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n- **II.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=4$\n- **III.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n\n- Statement II is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$ (not $4$).\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=2$ (not $3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "34dded57-bc20-5c74-8a7c-26db7d4b2c49"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2674be6f-e836-53b6-ad75-f49bcd215297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482879} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdaf3df-3466-5a46-9d91-de545d17a174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482896} {:answer/id #uuid "628e0240-3161-5a31-bec3-d0d78a344f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482984} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9bbb01-90f1-5914-b05b-4fdbb5c155e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483149} {:answer/id #uuid "d68f1746-c721-55d0-abfa-7820321d3544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482879} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d524d4c-bdb9-5865-a9c3-811956ada022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b149c895-74ab-53c4-8e34-ba4f249fb566" :question/math-academy-id "q-79314" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "60bb4227-9814-5e8d-9517-43a71cd69291" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f4d151-1cd0-5621-ba89-6470f4af573e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480746} {:answer/id #uuid "86ae4f21-6981-5f72-90e2-a3f32de9f3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481000} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca35bb-c3b8-5e45-a1f8-dfab2c842147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481008} {:answer/id #uuid "9c56e737-b927-5ce7-a48f-7ae0b922a01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481039} {:answer/id #uuid "bb990b9f-4a58-59bd-858f-c85000fc92e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481039} :answer-field/id #uuid "010bf50f-8de4-58ce-a0a7-3ef04178a4cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "656e0655-2fab-5e41-bca0-28f4bd148f34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35293" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\\})=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "c8698e91-362f-52ce-8632-aab433d3ae0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483440}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "841b51ab-4624-5135-9e62-a5d16f7f40d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118305} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5c3ff9-d114-5657-833d-571463f53ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118306} {:answer/id #uuid "0095d96f-0e33-50d5-bec2-0593e0bb2a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118307} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd230b8-acb7-514d-8c02-14cbb3854aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118308} {:answer/id #uuid "43fd4f00-8337-5638-b2ff-f732db2e10d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186118308} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe09fbe-4ea8-5884-b7ed-064efcb293bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c3ea0ae2-1531-5d26-b98b-bdd05134f6b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-34811" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "ca098e19-bec0-502e-83bc-ca18e83625bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483495}}] :activity/title "The Dimension of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482808}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079402} :activity/id #uuid "b34e1933-3814-529f-88c0-52e2e04c4df5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391 :topic/id #uuid "9b93c13d-3e57-5a78-b8a6-0f0a4d64062e" :topic/math-academy-id 614} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079402 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079670 :tutorial/content "A second-order, linear, homogeneous ordinary differential equation with constant coefficients has the general form\n\n$$\na\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + b\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + cy =0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nTo find solutions, we can propose a trial solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x},$ where $\\lambda$ is a constant to be determined. Differentiating this gives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the general ODE, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a({\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x})+b(\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x})+c({e}^{\\lambda x}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}(a{\\lambda }^{2}+b\\lambda +c) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{\\lambda x}$ can never be zero, the expression in the parentheses must be zero:\n\n$$\na\\lambda^2+b\\lambda + c =0 .\n$$\n\nThis is called the **characteristic equation** (or **auxiliary equation**). The roots of this quadratic equation for $\\lambda$ give us the solutions to the original differential equation.\n\nNext, we'll use the characteristic equation to solve a specific example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c8c3fdb1-a293-55b7-bb3f-a3b91cd13c06" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4046 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "70588abd-28cf-57e8-91ad-d7a423e0a722" :step/math-academy-id 24219 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079553}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078494 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078337 :question/id #uuid "c47d5c55-80f0-5093-810b-bf9c718c5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6587"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421926}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "8acd33b3-bbd7-5f1f-ae06-547799b4f66d" :step/math-academy-id 24220 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079404}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079404 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078495 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078338 :question/id #uuid "2065790f-428d-5477-ac77-0c5d07853143" :question/math-academy-id "e-6588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050522}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421928}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "3590f636-f3a1-5592-a64c-2fb248cb17db" :step/math-academy-id 24221 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079405}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078496 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078339 :question/id #uuid "1c7b0878-a386-5449-9ff4-c37c8cda1b92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503728}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "4f98b762-c792-57e5-8e73-6917892dea16" :step/math-academy-id 24222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079617 :tutorial/content "Let's solve the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2 y}{\\mathrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} + 6y =0\n$$\n\nFrom the previous slide, we know this ODE has a characteristic equation of the form $a\\lambda^2 + b\\lambda + c=0.$ Here, $a=1, b=5,$ and $c=6,$ so the characteristic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots are $\\lambda_1 = -2$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3.$ Each root gives a particular solution to the ODE: $y_1=e^{-2x}$ and $y_2=e^{-3x}.$\n\nThe **general solution** is a linear combination of these particular solutions:\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants." :tutorial/id #uuid "fe48b08c-e63b-577c-ba34-bc61bc5b2966" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14884 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "88086b0a-3e17-59ee-bc49-6709f897fb53" :step/math-academy-id 607934 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079403}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078644}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427573} :activity/id #uuid "227e8665-3923-5e3b-9f94-01de1d340546" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391 :topic/id #uuid "9b93c13d-3e57-5a78-b8a6-0f0a4d64062e" :topic/math-academy-id 614} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bec9d70-f8de-51cf-9e64-a849b809ce13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342607} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0ed665-7b86-5adb-85fc-2c91f4201111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343490} {:answer/id #uuid "c479827f-1f07-57f4-80cf-2fa9049345a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{6x}+B{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343966} {:answer/id #uuid "c65f01c2-eff5-56e3-8d94-e7a8e6fe2b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343978} {:answer/id #uuid "f51f0ae1-02ed-5b8f-a1ff-80e0e5ce8f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342607} :answer-field/id #uuid "c56404db-2432-58f9-a3d9-cc82f8d56420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b4af7b02-aa3b-5c89-83b9-f0034ff7803a" :question/math-academy-id "q-7781" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+8{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +4)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =-4.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c217041-6e07-5b2b-ab0b-44e2beb8265c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1ea764-4dca-5820-9034-d4b4f70f730b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355221} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae4ce25-9e97-5331-8ed8-96b2d9024430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355466} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba0b662-5fc6-5644-94c1-0e8b93d1e722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355900} {:answer/id #uuid "aff3c5d4-9583-5b46-a41e-d92a6fb9fbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356214} {:answer/id #uuid "d0936d80-a410-5f00-b078-134eaae76756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355900} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf81e32-4f57-597a-af29-291088b46e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb0ac50b-6a2d-5d53-997b-cb65e0ad2bb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-90720" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =1$ or $\\lambda =6.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "90c0dd24-f5ff-5d53-a9db-2ea11550684f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bda799f-2dc1-508a-b332-a2dc15ad6469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{-5t}-4{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427566} {:answer/id #uuid "463b0c74-3f5f-5ede-984f-20e81cf9dab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-5t}+6{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427567} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3822e6-4ea3-5bd8-88d6-1f35c1d51f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-5t}+2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427568} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd12f7d-a1de-5498-8a07-57a3b71dfca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427569} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c2bf7c-2f4c-5e0e-8a59-5fd6f0e6f6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427568} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1bdcac-c8f7-5ea7-bb80-b176a1307c71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "128c4108-f47c-515a-aaac-ed93eb92e39c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64708" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-10y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-10{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-5$ or $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-5t}$ and ${e}^{2t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $-3{e}^{-5t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ is not a solution."} :step/id #uuid "b72723e4-6cca-52eb-af62-fa6496045372" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427574}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427565}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172909} :activity/id #uuid "029bded9-b237-543e-8c70-78a922e3ce4f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048655 :topic/id #uuid "b2c456e2-ef0a-5a75-95da-68bb5f61da4a" :topic/math-academy-id 3356} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173163 :tutorial/content "A  plane curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r:[a,b]\\to \\Bbb R^2\n$$\n\nis said to be **simple** if it doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints. Notice that  this means that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t_1)\\neq \\mathbf r(t_2), \\qquad \na < t_1< t_2 < b.\n$$\n\nFor example, curve I shown below is simple while curve II is not simple.\n\n![](images/7b/7be1bc23d37c187a642d51917d6acb846147059bc6e9978d6ccd4ed35eed8794.png)\n\nA  plane curve is said to be **closed** if its terminal point coincides with its initial point, that is, if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(b)= \\mathbf r(a).\n$$\n\n![](images/4b/4b21a6e638cb8b0bae8967ba142fbfacb14349f34ff80d9359643e03ea1c1f83.png)\n\nFor example, curve III shown below is closed, while curve IV is not:\n\n![](images/85/8507fb039da42f37307ca3994cfb5ad66b288fb28d0cfd1a1a179acd2f83ae5e.png)\n\n**Watch out!** Curve III is an example of both a closed and simple curve. Notice that an example of a closed but *not* simple curve looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86dcd1dc864a2f0e2ceade072ba230b8fb4fe8923189a7eed2964f6f66a67651.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "72ccdc89-4807-5abb-9f69-7e017f195c3f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5100 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7bafdfe3-0f81-5668-ad79-241cf2fc2b91" :step/math-academy-id 32008 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172916}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172047 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171853 :question/id #uuid "0bf9999b-16dd-567b-9e52-e80a1b0c6a38" :question/math-academy-id "e-8675"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "325cfa6e-a3ff-51f3-9667-95e4ad86ac4c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503746}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Simple Curves"} :step/id #uuid "8a0f9d7f-ab53-56ab-bdd3-8439d8f9ce7a" :step/math-academy-id 32200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172917}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172048 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171854 :question/id #uuid "ee0133c9-056e-5595-b407-8f8f00c49823" :question/math-academy-id "e-8676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ca16ec8-183d-5e2e-9715-24107f8aa8c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503662}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closed Curves"} :step/id #uuid "b500b6d6-4ad6-5209-ba0d-8f0be8ba230d" :step/math-academy-id 32201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172049 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171855 :question/id #uuid "1b8cbd6a-e306-5092-a402-6a0da61c5ec8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8677"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337518a9-39a1-57e3-8686-74a135f7c3d7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503757}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Oriented Curves"} :step/id #uuid "01467366-cedf-5b66-ab24-41950e76376f" :step/math-academy-id 32202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173171 :tutorial/content "A very important (although very hard to prove) result in mathematics states the following:\n\nAny simple and closed curve divides the plane into exactly two regions, one inside the curve and one outside.\n\nBased on this fact, we can define the **orientation** of a simple closed curve as follows:\n\nA simple closed curve is **positively oriented** if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is **negatively oriented**.\n\nFor example, curve I shown below is positively oriented, while curve II is negatively oriented:\n\n![](images/95/9527dd8bdfd979a1d521ccf945e84ef9b557d67e3d89ce8f5e4f7bb8d287ee8c.png)\n\nAn alternative convenient way of looking at simple oriented curves is that a curve is positively oriented if it is traversed *counterclockwise* and it is negatively oriented if it is traversed *clockwise*." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c2c2591-a914-53a3-9f60-6e7335d049f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5165 :tutorial/title "Oriented Curves"} :step/id #uuid "2b76b9e7-6d5f-584c-837f-bd003bd3c835" :step/math-academy-id 32465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172918}}] :activity/title "Simple, Closed, and Oriented Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172178}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418068} :activity/id #uuid "efd4467a-4377-5bbc-a79e-4cb35382c6af" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048655 :topic/id #uuid "b2c456e2-ef0a-5a75-95da-68bb5f61da4a" :topic/math-academy-id 3356} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fb86e1-67a1-5051-8581-e7df8f55db54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9e/9e85303ac91d084f055bce35c874eaaab191a44602ce9223e5fda774b8491183.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411684} {:answer/id #uuid "5bacc02f-cc03-57e3-a071-b880e2b112dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/86/86d0a572024ecdbb9ea9d73b20db0710edb26313e5834b78ad48b494b1f09f69.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411726} {:answer/id #uuid "cadeec58-fb13-573b-93e4-7beb0073d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/71/71c3bcf20ac5e029c6e0033c0e69d7de04b5285bc68d5b7711c892d27d6eef9f.pn