:question/math-academy-id "q-43306" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ has two identical rows\n\n- **II.** One of the rows/columns of $A$ is proportional to another row/column of $A$\n\n- **III.** $A$ has two identical columns" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and III are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 8-4\\cdot 2=0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither two identical rows nor two identical columns.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "72bae4bc-a32a-5255-ba7a-a875edc0b957" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022ff781-0bb6-5222-8917-bb9fe088d40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} {:answer/id #uuid "68acb0f6-b335-5e47-b870-8fcc301ed02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415455} {:answer/id #uuid "a31adf10-60d1-51b5-9022-8cf1940c42be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415461} {:answer/id #uuid "ebaffa84-7e8a-5edc-8432-6adf9e5b2b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415465} {:answer/id #uuid "fae22a7a-c8b8-5a0b-bb2c-5bc815b3b48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ec239f-8a57-5ead-b4ff-042aa539477e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4554f29-f5fb-5556-852c-2f9d95422a4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-42464" :question/problem "Find $5det(A)+7det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has a zero column, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+7det(B)=5\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 0=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "96688804-9fe5-58e3-852b-6c7c9c3bf052" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "643b4a3a-7263-51ad-8b17-fdd82678cf99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464084} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e66fb8-1a18-56f4-8033-3baba4940db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464084} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ceb200f-e369-571b-b619-8f16a603488c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464976} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198009c8-d9e6-556e-aa1c-228a37b12311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464086} {:answer/id #uuid "da19cf9c-0b56-5a20-b004-c70d313f5184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464087} :answer-field/id #uuid "d98905ac-4493-5551-96b2-6d52f8e3f566" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464977} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ea76cf-d936-573e-a0be-56de7954b8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464088} {:answer/id #uuid "24e2aa19-faca-5a81-a238-e9e984e89ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464089} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed42e6d2-1aff-5708-ad8c-b45de0195450" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f60fdb9d-cf89-59bd-ad65-e9ec096b82df" :question/math-academy-id "q-140049" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n- **I.** $A$ is a triangular matrix\n- **II.** $A$ does not have identical rows\n- **III.** $A$ does not have proportional columns\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2=5\\ne 0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ is not a triangular matrix.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\nHence, if $|A|\\ne 0$ then $A$ doesn't have proportional nor identical rows/columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "9b5040f7-fe09-55f4-9bb3-3899218d89d2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d65d5f6-e3b7-5ee9-95cf-58c3e498a702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459975} {:answer/id #uuid "646a048e-f630-5013-95fe-6659b566a35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459976} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d3f262-127e-5585-b97c-20a126667044" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460854} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8460f2bb-e253-5210-9218-4e96e796beae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459977} {:answer/id #uuid "40f38a8d-b50e-505f-9411-5989253d0642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459977} :answer-field/id #uuid "cde9df91-1435-51c4-9805-9c117768203b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d90e2fb7-b1da-50a5-82da-f4e30f796fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459979} {:answer/id #uuid "8df48ba2-f889-595c-9590-a8404cf9b0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459980} :answer-field/id #uuid "f677a744-3bd0-564b-ade4-affd6abcf2a2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2d571c31-8e0e-520c-b34f-482cf5518003" :question/math-academy-id "q-42458" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n- **I.** $A$ has a zero column\n- **II.** $A$ has identical columns\n- **III.** $A$ has identical rows\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\n- $A$ has identical rows: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c48a0407-2623-5e3c-b4d7-9d37ae7f101c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4731d43a-1037-5467-97a5-e798a96ef9d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327555} {:answer/id #uuid "658a8d30-d9af-5e26-83da-9719b1081d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327818} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1056e6-0216-54a8-a348-f7fd16cd261c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327879} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4f71df-9ae0-539d-bc37-b9dfa1a2efd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328715} {:answer/id #uuid "f61627d1-d9a7-5fa0-8601-a797fc2e5d46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329093} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ecac406-0aef-584c-9d48-0a6c47d439e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a804e446-7433-50c7-a8ce-2769d7a93237" :question/math-academy-id "q-42459" :question/problem "Find $4det(A)+4det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- For the matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-1\\cdot 1=3.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has proportional columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4det(A)+4det(B)=4\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 0=12.$"} :step/id #uuid "e27b814b-a73a-54cb-83e5-018345109fb2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499602}}] :activity/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498725}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134811} :activity/id #uuid "1faaba32-288c-58f7-9e8b-36ad43309761" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/id #uuid "f6fd4776-56f0-5112-9cdc-83e344004d93" :topic/math-academy-id 2131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135102 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ expressed in terms of its component functions as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIf the first partial derivatives of $P$ and $Q$ exist, then the **divergence** of $\\mathbf F,$ denoted $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F,$ is the scalar given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we define the differential operator $\\nabla$ (a.k.a. the \"del\" operator) as \n\n$$\n\\nabla =  \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} +  \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is given by the dot product\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\, \\mathbf i + e^{yx} \\, \\mathbf j .\n$$\n\nThen, we can calculate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ using the dot product formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =(i\\,\\frac{∂}{∂x}+j\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y})\\cdot (2xy\\,i+{e}^{yx}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{yx}) \\\\  & =2y+x{e}^{yx}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "086f12c3-0ab5-54ed-8b4e-5c70e8c7c7e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5116 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5c2a161a-d267-5999-8795-ba9baba0146f" :step/math-academy-id 32111 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134849}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133692 :question/id #uuid "78e7cbb2-d207-5d5e-bf46-5c3b2686933f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8740"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424229}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"} :step/id #uuid "5d2f80d4-10e2-5fdc-b52f-7b5cadac99d2" :step/math-academy-id 32553 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133696 :question/id #uuid "ac6e7a2c-19a3-595a-ba46-f38c653a9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8927"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483395}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"} :step/id #uuid "eb39b82a-9c43-56ff-9c10-70ad9f735fab" :step/math-academy-id 33322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135114 :tutorial/content "Our goal now is to develop an intuitive understanding of what the divergence of a vector field measures.\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ and the point $S,$ shown below. Imagine that this vector field represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. \n\n![](images/a7/a785c84976242d699af11ab2203600e2007e95e35b60665ba9882c8f0f25bce3.png)\n\n \n\nWe can see from our diagram that $S$ acts like a **source**. You can think of this as an open faucet, pushing water onto the plane and away from itself. \n\nSince $S$ acts like a source, $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is nonzero. By convention, if a point $S$ acts like a source, the divergence is positive:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0\n$$\n\nIn this particular case, *every* point in our vector field acts as a source. So $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$ everywhere.\n\nIf we reverse the situation, we get the following:\n\n![](images/b4/b4c923bbde36568e4e2f36f7d388622a520d0799d558049f72d0194e0bd679f7.png)\n\n \n\nWe can see from our diagram that $T$ acts like a **sink**. You can think of this as an open plughole that sucks in the surrounding water.\n\nSince $T$ acts like a sink, $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is also nonzero. By convention, if a point $T$ acts like a sink, the divergence is negative:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0\n$$\n\nEvery point in our vector field acts like a sink in this case. So $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0$ everywhere.\n\nFinally, note that if $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of some vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is **solenoidal.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "a2cdf73b-116c-5cf4-a5a2-a8c95e447127" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5518 :tutorial/title "Physical Interpretation of Divergence"} :step/id #uuid "6a9298c4-106a-569c-bd0b-5d24d3e71b0f" :step/math-academy-id 34359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134848}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133705 :question/id #uuid "3cbc5b4a-dde4-59b0-bc34-08c4d8776e0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9489"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483377}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"} :step/id #uuid "77b5a013-eab6-50c3-9f63-2a51f2e967b2" :step/math-academy-id 35525 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135120 :tutorial/content "The following two vector fields behave either as a source or a sink. Therefore, they both have divergence.\n\n![](images/93/939efa85ebfe4805a27595b60a797b8a3435075590ee77f7a18d04bd76d91f1a.png)\n\nThe following vector fields are solenoidal (i.e., they each have zero divergence).\n\n![](images/ed/edb2f908fa78439bf985b5754c9fcc5968c21926dbe124297b67af046a9f70a3.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ddbe7ae2-caaf-5436-b1c7-aabc0eec115d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5793 :tutorial/title "Examples of Vector Field With Divergence and Solenoidal Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "88b27d23-7358-51e8-9457-a8b063ed5a52" :step/math-academy-id 35536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134826}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135121 :tutorial/content "There are a few things we should note about the divergence:\n\n- We can only compute the divergence of a *vector* field, and a vector field's divergence is a *scalar* field. So if $G(x,y) = \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F,$ then we have the following map:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf F(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}} \\mapsto\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,G(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{scalar field}}\n$$\n\nIn contrast, if $f(x,y)$ is a scalar field, then the statement $\\textrm{div}\\, f$ has no meaning.\n\n- We can easily extend the notion of divergence to three dimensions. So, if $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j + R\\,\\mathbf k,$ then $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} + \\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z},\n$$\n\nwhere the three-dimensional differential operator $\\nabla$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla =  \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} +  \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y} + \\mathbf k\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n- The divergence of a vector field at a point $P$ gives us helpful information about the tendency of the vector field to behave like a source or a sink at $P.$ We'll describe this in more detail shortly." :tutorial/id #uuid "9b8de083-51c2-581b-ad36-a773dca8af05" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5794 :tutorial/title "Some Important Notes About the Divergence of a Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "e856caa2-317e-5779-b004-597af558a506" :step/math-academy-id 35537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134822}}] :activity/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134010}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407634} :activity/id #uuid "23bb149c-27d7-5070-88b1-a3124f094ad2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/id #uuid "f6fd4776-56f0-5112-9cdc-83e344004d93" :topic/math-academy-id 2131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e054f8-7842-51d9-944e-506f086c64ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407614} {:answer/id #uuid "30bd69d5-9aed-5f53-bd57-88c0d8b71e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407615} {:answer/id #uuid "951b5e1a-06b7-5f54-b81b-2e7935528e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407621} {:answer/id #uuid "b2537554-7ead-5776-b7ea-b0a5c6ea2d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407622} {:answer/id #uuid "de576d13-a320-57ea-81fe-65cb1f522e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407614} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f12593-79dd-5e1b-8574-a974a94bfc5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f48c368c-417f-5ffe-9791-056b549ac545" :question/math-academy-id "q-105559" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,j+{e}^{2z}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(0,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2z}) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y+\\pi y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y+2{e}^{2z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(0,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(0,1,0) & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+0-\\pi +2 \\\\  & =2-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d73206f-cfb2-5389-b819-954a52ec5c0a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c79d74f-4409-57e4-b361-e941cc41aa8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407618} {:answer/id #uuid "941fb884-9dbd-5e6e-bdb3-9c13326a2f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407620} {:answer/id #uuid "d9966256-2b26-5637-969d-ec652f55ff58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407624} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0916bb-4bf2-5bfb-8625-f74c2a28d4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407626} {:answer/id #uuid "eecb625e-fb38-58c5-8c7d-425f0374e2c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j+3y{z}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407618} :answer-field/id #uuid "fafbd1ee-fb02-5785-b3f4-4426b308c48e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db7680f4-57f3-5a1a-a81a-52c92b75f829" :question/math-academy-id "q-105555" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,i-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+y{z}^{3}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "43f42226-1025-58c1-878e-7c4041f020a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "755d214e-8d2a-5a21-b9cf-f0df0ddc65e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437406} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfd0671-10c2-5e45-a69d-18a97c5bcb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437406} :answer-field/id #uuid "08fca85a-8f3f-5d04-ba2b-523fc3575ba4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6d7a93e-05d3-5b4e-a10d-309d06525f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437408} {:answer/id #uuid "cee06875-116c-5488-b531-455a557b12b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437408} :answer-field/id #uuid "77753f43-8be4-5002-8a52-0b36dbebd616" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438733} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0919152f-4318-5ee5-ab9e-853f1a97e081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437410} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd97fc8-8c06-56b4-a75e-62d1a96d9ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437411} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9e3e64-ecdb-5c87-af06-d417e36b04d6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438734} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98360c07-0eb0-5614-8178-f21e14480fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437412} {:answer/id #uuid "4b7d8c5d-3f07-50af-a145-77ac2e6077ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437413}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437412} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a0366b6-425d-5c79-b027-f2d81a491b79" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2847820b-e13e-563d-bfbb-bab640514e5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105572" :question/problem "![](images/58/584016c88e5916984e181118d52caab95dba1fd0d861e0fd248a4e023d1b07da.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- **III.** $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- **IV.** $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors decrease as we move horizontally and vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "84dd7364-713d-5340-9f9c-2da553617182"}] :activity/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407612}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436937} :activity/id #uuid "b0b7ba59-d2e5-5c0e-aa1c-0c290b8b930c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/id #uuid "f6fd4776-56f0-5112-9cdc-83e344004d93" :topic/math-academy-id 2131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05739988-eeed-5afd-a445-e1dc677cc92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437840} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf029c1-b1a8-5a8b-a676-cd13b44e113b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437840} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d7c78f-dac2-5b80-9554-1e05a93f2f93" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438949} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe246435-fd14-59a9-9160-df256f6ffb82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437842} {:answer/id #uuid "ff59cb0e-83ac-53c9-bb11-897ad674049d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437843}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437843} :answer-field/id #uuid "b39164a9-c699-5980-8ef6-6758528f95e7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438950} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1885359c-b7e4-5e24-b124-f8d267e8840f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437844} {:answer/id #uuid "cccdf236-c391-54e1-b0dc-8e77683dd03d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437845}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437844} :answer-field/id #uuid "96047e81-3269-5f84-bfb2-854075b3c2f8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438951} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c977abab-50be-5c44-961a-335884131fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437846} {:answer/id #uuid "209c01dc-3c0e-5ef6-b90f-25c2ce076ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437846} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ffbe2f7-9aa4-5310-9d23-ad54db885413" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5777673f-cba1-5775-ae01-f18d4531f239" :question/math-academy-id "q-131195" :question/problem "![](images/39/39dfec02c787141d8a728381f4093dfe9cbf3fdbf996177ba6f24d973015dc5f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\n- **II.** $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\n- **III.** $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\n- **IV.** $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0c687dc8-83c3-51c4-b6a7-2402b933a9ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241080 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3758688-c398-5563-9ef4-6725bb756a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437422} {:answer/id #uuid "20c2906c-786b-5d44-a2ab-80b29dc77300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437423} :answer-field/id #uuid "19f46c09-a764-5deb-8de8-68af7432ea43" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc20a89d-06eb-52ee-876e-878056126986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437424} {:answer/id #uuid "af5eb708-473f-56c4-b243-76dd14db4f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437424} :answer-field/id #uuid "72a39fa9-d231-5fb4-8edd-c467842038cf" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438741} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15199d6a-677b-53ea-8492-a7aa4a7eb581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437426} {:answer/id #uuid "19d090b2-e9f5-5f83-9d51-046d10ff6235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437427}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437426} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5999586-c6a2-5268-a3ec-230c0f2be240" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438742} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4882a295-c5c0-51e1-926e-f95e716e654e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437428} {:answer/id #uuid "9067697b-3c5f-57eb-bc0d-1edcb637afcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437429} :answer-field/id #uuid "564a4db5-ce17-5a9f-8f0c-cf41329af0c2" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2172851-a396-51c3-bd4e-4e1278b37143" :question/math-academy-id "q-105575" :question/problem "![](images/11/11d38951b884f17616a61403d793322d0e2ea28910e806fe8f018251508352a1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\n- **II.** $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\n- **III.** $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\n- **IV.** $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0eb2dca7-e256-5a91-aeab-a6e0be87d07f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436938}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a914c9-ac5b-510b-8770-498ebbd342d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436921} {:answer/id #uuid "88e03628-9e8e-5879-9ede-a96902f3797a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436926} {:answer/id #uuid "9a07bd93-8453-5012-b970-faf3a1a28f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3yz+2{y}^{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436927} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d3d9a8-1c50-5ad8-967c-67a2b8bdc976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xyz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+2yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436928} {:answer/id #uuid "d674ed52-351d-5f4a-9c10-e0caa107251f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4yz+2{y}^{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436930}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436926} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e44755-6b9a-5cca-b1ed-0c837e17c6d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "22c40f40-e4e5-529d-a83f-3f46f140c8db" :question/math-academy-id "q-105557" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=xyz\\,i+{y}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,j+y{z}^{2}\\,k.$ \nFind $\\text{div}\\,F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+2yz \\\\  & =3yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6a7d78a0-8d6d-590f-8d8a-84c35fdbd7e4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ab80cc-c857-5dfb-9a3a-cfdfd3d6fc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436922} {:answer/id #uuid "335bd84d-490e-5a69-979b-a11b772a9908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436923} {:answer/id #uuid "36f39977-9420-5792-a25e-40ed825c033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436924} {:answer/id #uuid "78e9e533-d5f7-5054-82d6-9d333edf1c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(1+y+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436925} {:answer/id #uuid "af0cee1b-58b3-5b9b-a7e7-5c521761b047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(2-y-z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436924} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf3222d-f787-5312-b008-17567e5ae0c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "36837fe3-6724-5929-9249-1bb3cf0a5e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131175" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={e}^{2x}\\,i-y{e}^{2x}\\,j-z{e}^{2x}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y{e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-z{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "af33c61a-5271-559b-9054-88db8d041045" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0743951f-f683-50d5-9001-7d4a2dc711a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437848} {:answer/id #uuid "893474df-f11f-5036-9840-620d813e6c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437848} :answer-field/id #uuid "f75f2c7d-45a9-5c61-b5c3-f30297ac41ee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438953} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d97ff95-b130-57f5-8f11-ba829a58042b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437850} {:answer/id #uuid "9997c21e-c21d-5bda-94da-7d904de42ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437851}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437850} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc505788-fd6f-5033-bb84-8f1d78e4ca57" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438954} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edd4f604-7a68-5b26-8157-40c68b325c96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437852} {:answer/id #uuid "69ed5f1f-94f0-51da-9619-cceadf43bb7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437853} :answer-field/id #uuid "1045e183-6def-5a6d-a0ce-4bb0bd1b936f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438955} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94d7c261-91e0-5aac-bbce-caaa5f7ca8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437854} {:answer/id #uuid "73b3ca58-410c-5e77-a2fb-219e1df21f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437854} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d01df11-c07e-5e3b-a901-c578fe72ec74" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9278ec55-4908-5c1c-8a74-640611473537" :question/math-academy-id "q-131196" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bf8de7e8719545b3d8abf5dc9fb4bc0c5f71034dba7186b7d9822d9e8be044d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\n- **II.** $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\n- **III.** $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\n- **IV.** $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $x$-components decrease as we move horizontally towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c4ea5422-7fe4-544c-96ca-1a8f6b528092"}] :activity/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436920}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483488} :activity/id #uuid "13de39d3-3bb8-598c-9ef9-38c2d90d7a15" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/id #uuid "f6fd4776-56f0-5112-9cdc-83e344004d93" :topic/math-academy-id 2131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e8ce4b-4397-5131-8016-ac24d8139492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482848} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0cb281-c41d-56fc-b840-37c1e1e299b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482947} {:answer/id #uuid "86a9b14f-682a-56ff-b5d7-630ab6d0e48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483046} {:answer/id #uuid "8c82e88d-36a4-5cf5-b6fa-804eb0316710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483060} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ee5433-5dac-5d77-ba1c-0052b43bbfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483060} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb66c9a3-57be-589b-8044-dbc471e3b934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aefd685f-6837-5195-a54c-56ec619b88d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105574" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1dd3e659d2580103232fa68620c60f493477649e1c4a8880023e6c6de88a167.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components decrease as we move vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "05100288-b9ed-59fd-b7a9-9a2592fbbb50" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483450}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483443 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10bcdd62-0b56-5da2-a50a-586faa6696b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy{z}^{2}+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263155} {:answer/id #uuid "295ca0f4-57e7-5a42-ac1c-c9565fd958a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y{z}^{2}+xy{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263355} {:answer/id #uuid "2be15acb-dbc4-5b00-98a8-38e26089e1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263371} {:answer/id #uuid "40fe9bd6-26ef-51ba-a8ee-4aa3a8d37085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y{z}^{2}+xy+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263576} {:answer/id #uuid "fe783955-ec8b-51ba-88fd-5f0cc372a593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+{e}^{y}+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263371} :answer-field/id #uuid "6647713f-61bd-5d5d-9904-3d926c79511c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "01f7efc1-008b-5b39-b852-37db53b31085" :question/math-academy-id "q-131176" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=2xy{z}^{2}\\,i+x{e}^{y}\\,j+y{z}^{3}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =2y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}+3y{z}^{2} \\\\  & =5y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b54e950-07e6-508f-8bbf-26f7181e85f0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71cbe9b9-abfb-59ee-9a3b-f7f689c26688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257781} {:answer/id #uuid "88b7f1be-ad85-5371-a6af-84faa05bd3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257981} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c60dec-38f0-52c7-b0aa-6a4369234d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258609} {:answer/id #uuid "d40a47e0-a307-5498-8db8-91105ba44fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258639} {:answer/id #uuid "edb9593d-e1cc-5ea7-ae77-b57065d0d67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258838}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258639} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c01b0b-506b-5b5b-95a1-7bc8278302dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74e7bf88-8da6-528c-84d5-ded22deb1ee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123200" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(x-y)^{3}\\,i+(y+z)^{3}\\,j+(x+z)^{3}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-y)^{2}+3(y+z)^{2}+3(x+z)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,2,2) & =3((-2)-(2))^{2}+3((2)+(2))^{2}+3((-2)+(2))^{2} \\\\  & =3(-4)^{2}+3(4)^{2}+3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2e0d18ce-acf0-5440-b4c0-fe1c45eb2401" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483443}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43bc396a-d9ed-5bb3-b93d-0fb796ec2097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482927} {:answer/id #uuid "516c5078-1161-5f0b-8b9d-0636463d00b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482956} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6cb7f0-12ce-523b-a991-3f45078b3156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483028} {:answer/id #uuid "87d47221-a635-52a3-99d0-f5a56e12e0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483050} {:answer/id #uuid "94b6c747-e673-5cf6-a43f-ad3df20d8406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483028} :answer-field/id #uuid "22420a9a-1575-5698-aa34-04729efa2a61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3aad6c37-ff96-5a6c-89b9-a9f32a30b3d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105570" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b64c156519b685acf390c51c1ea3e1540d82e0f7e047cf2f804d38a23956604.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the length of the vectors' $x$-components increase as we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9e3f811c-54a6-5c26-bdfa-8aaa897c63e3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a8a0905-3f5a-57f9-b71d-c2127323b463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482943} {:answer/id #uuid "5b08ebea-7284-5412-99de-e04b22ab6d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482971} {:answer/id #uuid "5db37158-5959-5928-a4a3-8612494e2e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482973} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1781db-53be-5369-8346-96df148295ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483025} {:answer/id #uuid "d185a6d1-cc28-52e1-8c76-58d00373669d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483162} :answer-field/id #uuid "346d1a8e-e297-5ff1-8dff-7d5740531d20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75ca7d55-6e3f-58c8-8a50-805ce5d1a756" :question/math-academy-id "q-41838" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{3}y\\,j+z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})) \\\\  & =2xyz+{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-1,-1,2) & =2(-1)(-1)(2)+(-1)^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =4-1+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "adea8609-2822-5adc-b454-084b01390c94" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483483}}] :activity/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482799}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125326} :activity/id #uuid "97435fa2-2414-5733-b9c9-109a6d98298e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049882 :topic/id #uuid "1ad374f3-e4f4-5dcc-ab6d-d6aed0790f3a" :topic/math-academy-id 1900} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125753 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$ with the plane $z=4.$\n\nTo find the intersection, we set both equations equal to each other:\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 4\n$$\n\nGraphing the above equation in the $xy$-plane, we get a circle $\\color{black} C$ with center at $(0,0)$ and radius $2.$\n\n![](images/c3/c32f35ccd2c0eb01732194ac419fa328f62f1281c8bf4fb5ccc75218153c46d5.png)\n\nThe curve  $\\color{black} C$ is called a **level curve** of the surface $z = x^2 + y^2.$ The level curves of the form\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = c\n$$\n\nfor $c=1,2,3,4$ are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/6c/6c86604edc24e92fe51a8eed55386d8b01531b4a4f025e70f7ff33caf0c5df81.png)\n\nThe diagram above is called a **contour plot.** The level curves are sometimes called **contours.**\n\nIn general, a **level curve** $L_{c}(f)$ of a real-valued function of two variables $f(x,y)$ is any set of the form\n\n \n\n$$\nL_{c}(f) = \\left\\{(x,y) \\, : \\,f(x,y)=c\\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a constant.\n\n**Note:** In our example, $x^2 +y^2 \\ge 0$ for all $(x,y),$ which means that the sets $L_{c}(f)$ are nonempty for $c \\ge 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3a86e054-2c30-5b05-8ec7-9293b3a78aa8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2876 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "76440382-82ba-5d62-8fa4-df79b4f447e7" :step/math-academy-id 15418 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125456}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124287